Metric Operator Breakdown: Individual Metric-Only Benchmark Findings

Executive Summary

This page documents the individual metric-operator benchmarks completed against the 100% prepared datasource using the current known-good execution baseline.

Scope

These are metric-only runs.

They do not include the upstream Map operators from the full Model Plan. The purpose of the exercise is therefore to understand performance inside the Metrics layer, not to attribute a percentage of total end-to-end Model Plan runtime to any individual operator.

Test baseline

• Same prepared 100% datasource
• Same Spark / Dataproc environment
• Same SK-mig12-MF06 job profile
• Manual partitioning at 20,000
• spark.memory.fraction = 0.6
• Runs executed individually rather than concurrently

Main findings

1. gen_metric_1 is the most expensive operator in the tested group.
2. Five of the six operators independently generate a second, shuffle-heavy Stage 2 with substantial spill and long-running straggler tasks.
3. fv4_metric_8 is the clear exception. It completes after one downstream stage and avoids the heavy second-stage spill pattern.
4. Metric count alone does not explain runtime. Metric design and execution shape are more important.
5. The recurring expensive pattern points toward the underlying metric definitions, including Group By keys, windows, aggregation type and resulting partition distribution, rather than simply the number of metrics configured.
6. Several runs experienced INTERNAL_ERROR_NETWORK on Stage 1 before successful retry. Successful-stage metrics are still useful for operator comparison, but end-to-end elapsed time should be treated as slightly contaminated by infrastructure instability.

