10. Metric Count Is Not a Linear Cost Predictor

A simple count-based interpretation would imply that more metrics should result in proportionally more runtime.

The benchmarks do not support that.

Examples:

• gen_metric_3: 12 metrics -> ~61 minutes
• frdrt_metric_7: 22 metrics -> ~71 minutes
• fv4_metric_8: 25 metrics -> only ~40 minutes
• fv2_metric_6: 44 metrics -> ~79 minutes

fv4_metric_8 contains more than twice as many metrics as gen_metric_3, yet it executes materially faster.

Therefore:

> **Metric design matters more than metric count alone.**

The computational cost is likely driven by combinations of:

• Group By key(s)
• key cardinality / distribution
• aggregation type
• window length
• amount of state maintained
• amount of data redistributed by shuffle
• number of metrics sharing / not sharing the same execution pattern

────────

11. Infrastructure Stability Observation

Five of the six individual runs encountered a Stage 1 INTERNAL_ERROR_NETWORK failure before successfully retrying.

This should be treated as a separate platform stability workstream.

The repeated network failures create additional elapsed-time overhead and should not be interpreted as metric logic failures.

For performance comparison, the successful stage attempts remain useful.

For end-to-end runtime comparison, the retry overhead should be explicitly noted.

────────

12. Current Engineering / Data Science Problem Statement

The individual operator benchmarks support the following problem statement:

> **Five of the six tested Metrics operators independently generate a second shuffle-heavy stage with approximately 1.2–1.37 TiB of shuffle input, 124–152 GiB of memory spill, 7–10 GiB of disk spill and 33–45 minute task tails. `gen_metric_1` is the most expensive tested operator. `fv4_metric_8` is materially cheaper and does not generate the same heavy second stage.**

The next investigation should therefore compare the metric definitions within the expensive operators against fv4_metric_8.

Specifically review:

• Group By keys
• window durations
• aggregation type
• high-cardinality fields
• countDistinct usage
• long-window metrics
• repeated / duplicate metric calculations
• whether each metric is actually consumed downstream
• model/business value of expensive metrics

────────

13. Recommended Priority

Priority 1: gen_metric_1

Review first because it currently has:

• longest overall runtime
• longest Stage 1
• highest final shuffle read
• highest final memory spill
• highest final disk spill

Priority 2: Compare gen_metric_1 with fv4_metric_8

This is the highest-value structural comparison.

Question:

> **What grouping/window/aggregation pattern exists in `gen_metric_1` that creates the second shuffle-heavy stage, but is absent or materially different in `fv4_metric_8`?**

Priority 3: Rationalise metrics by value and cost

Do not remove metrics based only on count.

Prioritise:

> **high computational cost + low/zero downstream value**

before low-cost metrics that may still be useful.

